PROPOSED CHANGE, NOT APPLIED -- the thermal display intent
==========================================================

STATUS: PROPOSAL. Nothing in this patch is applied to the working tree, and
nothing under sdk/ was written by any lane that produced it. The control-room
server was running throughout and was not restarted, signalled or touched.

Prepared by:   heat-transfer lab-lane, 2026-09-01 (first cut), corrected the
               same night by a second heat-transfer lab-lane after the first
               cut was measured against the files that actually landed.
Base:          cut against sdk/chief_engineer/router.py at blob
               40521daec147bf19d2f2ee8dfe059bd4da8cae5e and
               sdk/chief_engineer/scope.py as at the same commit. Confirm the
               base has not moved under you before reading further:
                 git rev-parse HEAD:sdk/chief_engineer/router.py
                 git rev-parse HEAD:sdk/chief_engineer/scope.py
                 git apply --check docs/campaigns/T-family/demo/PROPOSED_thermal_intent.patch
               If a blob differs, the patch may still apply; the third
               command is the operative test, the first two tell you whether
               somebody has edited those files since this was written.
Reviewers:     heat-transfer supervisor (reads it as a diff, SUPERVISION §3
               check 1), then the cfd team, because sdk/chief_engineer/ is
               shared control-room tooling owned by neither team and the
               routing table it changes governs every team's prompts.
Design note:   docs/campaigns/T-family/demo/PROPOSED_thermal_intent_NOTE.md
               Read the note first. It carries the measured before/after
               routing table, the two prompts to type, and the restart
               procedure.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
  1. Adds a `thermal-display` intent to the router: a thermal question about a
     body whose run has ALREADY LANDED is answered by presenting that run.
  2. Adds `sdk/workflows/thermal_display.py`, which presents landed figures,
     tables and instrument checks. It starts no solver, writes no case, queues
     no job and books no compute.
  3. Adds `THERMAL_DISPLAY` to `_SURFACE_KEEPS_ROUTE`. This is load-bearing,
     not cosmetic: the thermal acts open with an upload, and without it
     `apply_surface` converts the route into a geometry study and points the
     incompressible aerodynamic chain at a body someone asked a thermal
     question about. Measured: at HEAD, both act prompts with an uploaded
     surface become `geometry-study`.
  4. Adds ONE ROW to `CAPABILITIES` in `sdk/chief_engineer/scope.py`. Without
     it no scope-down line can ever fire for this act, because scope.py:85
     says an intent absent from that table "is NOT assumed incapable: it is
     simply not checked". The row is `frozenset({THERMAL, UNSTEADY})` and the
     comment beside it records the one coarseness it carries: the table is
     keyed on the intent while the two acts behind that intent are not equally
     capable. See the note.

WHAT THE CORRECTION FIXED, AND WHY IT MATTERED
  The first cut of this patch was measured against the files on disk and Act A
  would have shown Sanaa a BLANK. Two independent causes, both now repaired
  in `sdk/workflows/thermal_display.py`:

  (a) FILENAME CASE. The first cut named `acta_screen_data.json`,
      `acta_radial_profile`, `acta_envelope` and two figures that were never
      built. The files that landed are `actA_*` with a capital A, and ext4 is
      case-sensitive, so every path missed. The screen set now names the five
      Act A figures that are at HEAD -- `actA_map_table`, `actA_envelope`,
      `actA_radial_profile`, `actA_monitor_replay`, `actA_assumption_beat` --
      each one checked with `git cat-file -e HEAD:<path>` one at a time.

  (b) AN INCOMPATIBLE BUNDLE SCHEMA, and this was the serious one. Act C's
      bundle carries `source_case`, `reader`, `mesh`, `instrument_check_*`,
      `per_cell_table`, `shared_colour_scale_K`; Act A's carries NONE of them.
      It carries `map_rows`, `envelope`, `radial`, `monitors`,
      `anchor_checks`, `numerical_uncertainty` -- and its planted-zero results
      under `planted_zero_controls`.

      The consequence was not cosmetic. The module looked for
      `instrument_check_*` only, so for Act A the ENTIRE INSTRUMENT-CHECK
      TABLE WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENTLY SKIPPED: a screen full of temperatures
      with no evidence that the readers behind them can see anything at all.
      That is a CLAUDE.md rule 3 failure, and silence is its worst form.

      The repair is a NORMALISER, not a second copy of the presentation code.
      `_normalise` translates whichever dialect arrived into one internal
      shape; everything below it is dialect-blind. `_instrument_rows` reads
      BOTH spellings, and Act A's `anchor_checks` get their own table with
      their own column names, because an anchor check is not a planted zero
      and must not be dressed as one.

  (c) THE REFUSAL. If no recognisable control block is found in a bundle, the
      act now REFUSES: it says so on screen, names what it looked for and what
      the bundle actually carried, draws no figure, quotes no number and
      returns 2 -- the same refuse-rather-than-degrade discipline the T-family
      comparators use. A verification block that renders as nothing is worse
      than one that renders as "not recorded", and both are worse than a
      refusal.

  (d) NAMED ABSENCES. Each screen set carries a `not_available` table listing
      the beats that were asked for and cannot be shown, with the reason. For
      Act A: A3 geometry and meshing, A4 the feasibility-first framing, A6.1
      the temperature field and the velocity panel, A6.6 the operating
      recommendation, A8 the assumptions box, A9 the export. For Act C: C6.3
      the coolant outlet temperature (the channels are not resolved as a fluid
      region, so there is no coolant stream to have an outlet) and C7 the
      transitional-flow sensitivity band (no run on disk supplies it). Nothing
      is approximated and nothing is quietly omitted.

  (e) TWO PENDING FIGURES, DECLARED AS PENDING. `actA_temperature_field` and
      `actA_velocity_field` were being generated by another lane while this
      correction was written; they are on disk untracked and are NOT at HEAD.
      They are declared with status PENDING: if they are on disk when the act
      runs they go up and their not-available rows are dropped; if they are
      not, the two A6.1 rows stand and say so. Neither outcome is silent and a
      reviewer of this file can see both. MEASURED both ways -- 7 plots with
      them present, 5 without.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT DO
  * It does not add, remove or edit a single character of
    `_OUT_OF_SCOPE_DOMAINS`. The refusal list is untouched. See the note for
    why no edit to it is needed, and for the measured evidence that the list is
    consulted only on the no-workflow fallback path.
  * It does not solve anything, queue anything, or invoke a solver.
  * It does not touch `server.py`, `control_room.html`, or any other module.

WHAT THE ROUTER STILL REFUSES AFTER THIS PATCH
  Every prompt refused at HEAD is refused after this patch, unchanged, because
  the refusal path is unchanged. Specifically, a thermal request that names no
  body this lab has actually run scores nothing on the new intent and keeps the
  route it has today. Measured, still reaching the refusal path after the
  patch:
    "Do a thermal analysis of the motor and tell me the peak temperature."
    "Nusselt number on the cooling channel."
    "Simulate boiling in the coolant loop."
    "Simulate how the cube melts in a fire."
    "Model the combustion in the chamber."
  A KNOWN PRE-EXISTING DEFECT THIS PATCH DOES NOT FIX, and the reviewers must
  see it: three thermal prompts that SHOULD refuse do not refuse at HEAD
  either, because they route to `unseen-geometry`, which has a workflow, so the
  refusal list is never consulted for them. They behave identically before and
  after:
    "Run a conjugate heat transfer study on a rocket nozzle I am designing."
    "Do a thermal stress analysis of the turbine blade."
    "Model the heat transfer on the wing."
  That is a defect in the refusal ARCHITECTURE, not in the refusal LIST, and it
  is not this patch's to repair. It is written up in the design note for the
  cfd and verification teams.

MEASURED AFTER THE CORRECTION, in a scratch copy of the repo tree at HEAD with
this patch applied, driving `thermal_display.main()` in a throwaway
interpreter. The real tree was never patched and the live server was never
touched.
